---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="JWT Keys">
  <h1>JWT Keys</h1>
  <p>
    Identity signs access tokens with RS256. The signing backend is
    selected by <code>GATEWAY_JWT_SIGNER</code> — either <code>file</code>
    (the default) or <code>kms_aws</code>. The file signer loads its keys
    from a JSON document at the path given in
    <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE</code>. If that path is empty the container
    generates a throwaway in-memory key (with <code>kid</code>
    <code>dev</code>) on every start and logs
    <code>jwt_signer_dev_key_generated</code> as a warning — never use this
    in production.
  </p>
  <p>
    The AWS KMS backend (<code>GATEWAY_JWT_SIGNER=kms_aws</code>) instead
    reads <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KMS_KEYS</code> and
    <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KMS_AWS_REGION</code>; see the KMS docs for that path.
    The rest of this page covers the file backend.
  </p>

  <h2>Format</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE</code> points at a JSON file whose top-level
    value is an object with a <code>keys</code> array. Each entry has a
    <code>kid</code>, a PEM-encoded RSA private key
    (<code>private_key_pem</code>), and optional <code>not_before</code> /
    <code>expires_at</code> RFC 3339 timestamps (<code>""</code> or omitted
    means no bound). There is no <code>active</code> flag — the active
    signing key is the entry with the latest <code>not_before</code> that
    is currently in force (<code>not_before &lt;= now &lt; expires_at</code>);
    ties break by file order. All keys are published to JWKS so
    previously-issued tokens continue to verify.
  </p>
  <Code code={`{
  "keys": [
    {
      "kid": "k-2026-05",
      "not_before": "2026-05-01T00:00:00Z",
      "expires_at": "2026-08-01T00:00:00Z",
      "private_key_pem": "-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nMIIE...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n"
    },
    {
      "kid": "k-2026-04",
      "not_before": "2026-04-01T00:00:00Z",
      "expires_at": "2026-07-01T00:00:00Z",
      "private_key_pem": "-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nMIIE...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n"
    }
  ]
}`} lang="json" title="keys.json" />
  <p>
    The parser accepts both PKCS#1 (<code>-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----</code>)
    and PKCS#8 (<code>-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----</code>) — but only RSA keys.
    The signer is constructed by <code>buildFileSigner</code> in
    <code>identityserver/adapters.go</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Generating a key</h2>
  <Code code={`# 2048-bit RSA key, PKCS#1 PEM
openssl genrsa -out k-2026-05.pem 2048

# Build the keys file and point GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE at it
PEM=$(awk '{printf "%s\\\\n", $0}' k-2026-05.pem)
cat > keys.json <<EOF
{"keys":[{"kid":"k-2026-05","not_before":"2026-05-01T00:00:00Z","private_key_pem":"$PEM"}]}
EOF

export GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE=/path/to/keys.json`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>JWKS endpoint</h2>
  <p>
    Identity exposes the public half of every key in the ring at
    <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code>. Downstream services should fetch
    this once, cache it, and use it to verify access tokens locally.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -s http://identity/.well-known/jwks.json`} lang="bash" />
  <Code code={`{
  "keys": [
    {"kty":"RSA","use":"sig","alg":"RS256","kid":"k-2026-05","n":"...","e":"AQAB"},
    {"kty":"RSA","use":"sig","alg":"RS256","kid":"k-2026-04","n":"...","e":"AQAB"}
  ]
}`} lang="json" />

  <h2>Rotation</h2>
  <p>
    See <a href="/identity/docs/examples/key-rotation">Key Rotation</a> for
    the full step-by-step. The short version:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Generate a new key with a fresh <code>kid</code>.</li>
    <li>Add it to the file with a future <code>not_before</code> so it
      publishes to JWKS but does not yet sign; reload.</li>
    <li>Wait long enough for downstream services to refresh JWKS.</li>
    <li>Once the new key's <code>not_before</code> has passed it becomes the
      active signer automatically (latest in-force <code>not_before</code>
      wins); set an <code>expires_at</code> on the old key.</li>
    <li>After every old token has expired (≥ <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code>), drop the retired key from the file.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Verification</h2>
  <p>
    <code>VerifyAccessToken</code> in <code>pkg/jwt/token.go</code> reads the
    <code>kid</code> header, looks the key up in the ring, then verifies the
    signature, <code>exp</code>, and <code>iss</code>. Tokens with an
    unknown <code>kid</code>, expired <code>exp</code>, or wrong issuer are
    rejected.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
